## moss-terminal.install — pacman lifecycle messages.
##
## Deliberately inert: this file only prints. It runs as root, so it must
## never touch a user's $HOME, never call systemctl/useradd/pacman, and never
## reach the network. Everything below is something the *user* runs later,
## as themselves.

_moss_notes() {
	cat <<'EOF'

Moss Terminal is installed.

  Launch it        moss-terminal          (or the "Moss Terminal" desktop entry)
  Standalone CLI   moss                   (same engine, plain terminal agent)

--- 1. Configure an AI provider (required before 》 does anything) ----------

  moss config          interactive configuration TUI
  moss paths           print exactly which files the engine will read/write
  moss models          list the models available from the active provider

  There is also a guided four-preset wizard (opencode Zen / DeepSeek / OpenAI /
  custom OpenAI-compatible), but NOTE: this package does not install it. It
  lives in the project's git checkout only, so you need to clone the repo:

  python3 scripts/moss-setup.py     # from a source checkout; NOT installed

  Configuration is a normal file if you prefer an editor:

  ~/.config/moss/config.jsonc     providers, active_provider, prompt/persona
  ~/.config/moss/.env             API keys, referenced as "$env:VAR" from
                                  config.jsonc. Create it mode 0600.

  A running terminal picks up config changes without a restart:

  printf '\033]7717;reload-config\007'

--- 2. PRIVACY NOTICE, read before using a public provider -----------------

  The setup wizard offers "opencode Zen" (https://opencode.ai/zen/v1) as a
  zero-configuration, no-API-key-needed provider. It is a PUBLIC THIRD-PARTY
  ENDPOINT operated by someone other than this project.

  Moss Terminal's whole point is that the AI sees your terminal. Every
  question you ask sends the new terminal output since your last question --
  the commands you ran and everything they printed -- to the configured
  provider. In a real shell session that can include hostnames, file paths,
  usernames, internal URLs, git remotes, error messages containing tokens,
  and anything a command happened to echo.

  Do not point Moss Terminal at a public endpoint on a machine where that
  matters. Use your own API key with a provider you have a data agreement
  with, or a local OpenAI-compatible server. Nothing is sent anywhere until
  you have configured a provider and typed a 》 question.

--- 3. Your shell matters: bash is degraded --------------------------------

  zsh and fish are recommended. They are supported natively (a zsh
  accept-line hook and a fish \r binding), and your question text reaches the
  engine byte for byte.

  Under bash, 》 is intercepted through command_not_found_handle, which only
  ever sees the question AFTER bash has word-split it. Quotes, globs and
  runs of whitespace inside your question are eaten before Moss can see
  them. Simple questions work; anything containing shell metacharacters
  does not survive. This is a bash limitation, not a bug we can patch out.

  Also note (zsh and bash): if a request produces no output and you do not
  press Ctrl+C, the shell-side `read -t 300` gives up after 300 seconds and
  returns your prompt, so a network hang cannot wedge the terminal forever.
  fish's `read` has no timeout, so under fish use Ctrl+C. Ctrl+C cancels a
  streaming answer at any time in every shell.

--- 4. Where things live ---------------------------------------------------

  ~/.config/moss/         config.jsonc, .env, prompts/  (or $MOSS_HOME/config)
  ~/.cache/moss/logs/     engine logs                   (or $MOSS_HOME/cache)
  /usr/lib/moss-terminal/ the patched kitty payload
                          (engine: lib/kitty/libmoss.so)
  /usr/share/doc/moss-terminal/            project documentation
  /usr/share/moss-terminal/packaging/      opt-in kitty-sync integration

  Setting MOSS_HOME overrides every path above -- useful for sandboxing.

  Debugging a question that never answers:
  MOSS_LOG=debug, ask again, then read ~/.cache/moss/logs/.

  kitty's own man pages ship inside the payload rather than /usr/share/man,
  so they cannot collide with the system kitty package:
  MANPATH=/usr/lib/moss-terminal/share/man man kitty

  `kitten` is likewise inside the payload and is NOT on your PATH (that name
  belongs to the stock kitty package, and kitty does not add its own bin dir
  to PATH on Linux). Call it by full path:
  /usr/lib/moss-terminal/bin/kitten icat image.png

--- 5. Coexistence and updates --------------------------------------------

  This package does not touch the stock kitty package: no shared files, no
  /usr/bin/kitty, no /usr/bin/kitten. You can have both installed.

  REBUILD AFTER A PYTHON MINOR UPGRADE. The terminal links the libpython it
  was built against (currently libpython3.14.so.1.0). Arch rebuilds its own
  kitty on every python bump; an AUR package does not get one automatically.
  If moss-terminal ever dies with
    libpython3.X.so.1.0: cannot open shared object file
  just rebuild/reinstall this package. The `moss` CLI is unaffected.

  /usr/share/moss-terminal/packaging/ holds an OPTIONAL pacman hook and an
  OPTIONAL systemd *user* timer that notify you when Arch ships a newer
  kitty than this build was made against. Neither is installed or enabled --
  read that directory's README.md first. They are aimed at source checkouts;
  as an AUR user you normally just wait for the next package release.

EOF
}

post_install() {
	_moss_notes
}

post_upgrade() {
	cat <<'EOF'

Moss Terminal upgraded.

  Existing ~/.config/moss/config.jsonc is untouched by the package.
  If a running terminal should pick up a config change without restarting:
  printf '\033]7717;reload-config\007'

  Re-read the full notes any time with:
  pacman -Qi moss-terminal   (and /usr/share/doc/moss-terminal/)

EOF
}

post_remove() {
	cat <<'EOF'

Moss Terminal removed.

  Your personal data was NOT deleted -- pacman does not own it. If you want
  it gone, remove it yourself:

    rm -rf ~/.config/moss     # config.jsonc, .env (API KEYS), prompts, personas
    rm -rf ~/.cache/moss      # logs, model cache
    rm -rf ~/.local/state/moss 2>/dev/null   # session/memory state, if present

  ~/.config/moss/.env contains API keys in plaintext. Delete it if you are
  decommissioning the machine or handing it on.

  If you enabled the optional integrations by hand, undo them by hand:

    sudo rm -f /etc/pacman.d/hooks/moss-kitty-sync.hook
    systemctl --user disable --now moss-sync-check.timer
    rm -f ~/.config/systemd/user/moss-sync-check.{service,timer}

EOF
}
